WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.634 --> 00:00:02.869
 James, Erich, congratulations.

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:04.136
You're both in the final.

00:00:04.203 --> 00:00:06.406
Now you're both one step
closer to redemption,

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:08.441
the title of "Forged
in Fire" champion,

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:10.777
and a check for $10,000.

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:12.378
When you came here,
you used our tools

00:00:12.445 --> 00:00:15.381
and equipment to forge signature
blades in your signature style.

00:00:15.481 --> 00:00:17.483
Now we're sending you
back to your home forges

00:00:17.583 --> 00:00:20.620
to recreate an iconic
weapon from history.

00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:25.025
That weapon is--
a sword breaker.

00:00:28.594 --> 00:00:31.330
Used by knights across
16th-century Europe,

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.299
the sword breaker
is a unique dagger

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:36.669
designed to render enemies
defenseless on the battlefield.

00:00:36.735 --> 00:00:40.006
The weapon's sharp, comb-like
teeth could entrap and snap

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:42.241
an opponent's sword in
half, while its razor

00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:44.710
sharp edge and tip were
employed to deliver

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:46.312
fatal slashes or stabs.

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:48.414
The sword breaker was so
versatile in its ability

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.817
to disarm and counterattack,
it became the standard sidearm

00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:53.118
of its day.

00:00:53.185 --> 00:00:54.720
Today, it's immortalized
in the PlayStation

00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:56.322
game "Sword Art Online--

00:00:56.388 --> 00:00:58.023
Hollow Fragment,"
where it's wielded

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.159
by the character Philia.

00:01:00.226 --> 00:01:01.126
Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:02.328
We'll see you in five days.

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.001
ERICH: I'm back
in my home forge.

00:01:09.101 --> 00:01:11.070
Last time I was on,
I was sent out first.

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:13.372
So to make it through to
the finals, I'm elated.

00:01:13.439 --> 00:01:15.374
I'm assuming we're going to
try to break some swords,

00:01:15.441 --> 00:01:16.876
and I'm all right with that.

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:18.878
I'm actually thinking
of going with a club

00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:20.313
design for my teeth.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:21.781
It's giving them
a lot more mass,

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:23.982
a lot more weight, and a
lot more meat to bite into

00:01:24.049 --> 00:01:25.385
and to dig.

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:27.987
So hopefully that will
prove to be a good design.

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:30.122
Maybe someday it'll
end up in a museum.

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:32.425
I'm going to establish my
point and kind of narrow

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:35.294
that down, taking
the corners in.

00:01:35.361 --> 00:01:38.197
I'm making a lot more
progress than I expected,

00:01:38.263 --> 00:01:39.765
but the hard part's coming up.

00:01:39.832 --> 00:01:43.703
The hard part's going to be the
meticulous parts of this build.

00:01:43.769 --> 00:01:44.771 line:7%
 Time for day two.

00:01:44.838 --> 00:01:45.938
First thing I'm
going to do is I'm

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:47.440
going to drill holes
and then finish

00:01:47.507 --> 00:01:50.276
cutting all of those teeth
in before heat treat.

00:01:50.343 --> 00:01:52.178
That looks pretty
wicked right there.

00:01:52.245 --> 00:01:54.280
I was originally going to cut
these into the spade look,

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.182
but the triangular
look is really badass.

00:01:57.282 --> 00:02:00.086
It's going to save me
a lot of file time.

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:03.189
Oh, so pretty.

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:04.990
I'm going to let this
forge warm up a little bit.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:06.292
I've got to get my quench tank.

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:08.594
Then I'll be ready
for a hardening cycle.

00:02:08.694 --> 00:02:10.262
I don't usually quench
blades this big,

00:02:10.362 --> 00:02:11.698
so it's going to be difficult.

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:13.133
All right.

00:02:13.199 --> 00:02:15.501
I'm heating up some scrap
material to heat the oil,

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:18.071
see if I can't get it
about 120 degrees in there.

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:19.905
You need to heat up the
oil because you don't want

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:21.808
to shock the steel
while hardening,

00:02:21.875 --> 00:02:25.444
because it will
potentially crack.

00:02:25.511 --> 00:02:26.279
Damn it.

00:02:26.379 --> 00:02:27.113
Damn it.

00:02:27.179 --> 00:02:28.680
What the [bleep] is going on?

00:02:28.781 --> 00:02:30.216
I've got to--

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:32.685
I'm just fixing to waste about
two gallons of [bleep] oil.

00:02:32.785 --> 00:02:34.687
[bleep]

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:38.824
The steel punches a hole in
the bottom of my PVC cap,

00:02:38.891 --> 00:02:40.326
and that [bleep] sucks.

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:42.061
I don't know what the
[bleep] to do now.

00:02:42.161 --> 00:02:44.063
This is going to set
me back a half day.

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:48.033 line:0%
I need to find a quench
tank, or I'm done.

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:49.468 line:7%
ERICH: I'm ready to get going.

00:02:49.535 --> 00:02:51.171
I'm ready to get the
teeth on the blade today.

00:02:51.237 --> 00:02:54.641
This is the most critical
part of the build, period.

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:57.576
This is the very first time
I've ever cut into a blade other

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:58.911
than a gut hook.

00:02:59.011 --> 00:03:00.512
I'm going to hot chisel them
out just to see how they look,

00:03:00.612 --> 00:03:02.515
making sure that that's
the right decision.

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:04.283
Of the designs I
have for my teeth,

00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:05.784
they could go drastically wrong.

00:03:05.884 --> 00:03:07.954
Each one of these teeth are a
potential spot for breaking,

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:12.224
so I have to be very meticulous
on where each placement goes.

00:03:12.324 --> 00:03:13.792
I think it's going to be mean.

00:03:13.859 --> 00:03:16.462
With the heat treat, I don't
know what to expect with it.

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:17.630
I truly don't.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.866
If there's any stress
left in the metal,

00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:21.233
it's going to come
out, and that is

00:03:21.333 --> 00:03:22.701
usually in the shape of a warp.

00:03:22.768 --> 00:03:25.337
That's got me a little
concerned, I got to say.

00:03:25.404 --> 00:03:27.206
If anything is going to
make me throw up today,

00:03:27.306 --> 00:03:28.875
it's going to be the heat treat.

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.085
That's really good.

00:03:39.185 --> 00:03:40.019
They're nice and straight.

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:40.986
They look terrific.

00:03:41.053 --> 00:03:42.355 line:7%
I'm happy.

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:44.223 line:0%
JAMES: I had to find
a different tank,

00:03:44.289 --> 00:03:46.125 line:0%
and finally I've
found a metal 5-gallon

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:48.060 line:7%
can and quenched my blade.

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:49.194
It looks really good.

00:03:49.261 --> 00:03:50.329
The details I'm
working on today is

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:53.032
I'm going to be
finishing the guard,

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.700
shape that up with some
files, make it nice

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:58.371
and round, shape my
handle, and do my test.

00:03:58.437 --> 00:04:00.573
Ooh, that's nice.

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:01.907
Already have a
piece of purpleheart

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:03.476
wood from my handles.

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.244
And my drift for making
the hole for the guard,

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:06.512
it's going to now
become my pommel.

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:11.884
Everything looks great.

00:04:11.984 --> 00:04:14.420
Now I have to get that primary
edge sharpened and then

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.888
do some cutting tests on it.

00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:18.424
I got my daughters out
there and my tortoise, Gio.

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:21.928
They want to see what
this blade can do.

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:23.295
When I was out in
the first round,

00:04:23.396 --> 00:04:24.797
my daughters were
both sad for me.

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:26.632
But having this second
opportunity to go back

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:28.601
and shown that if you
really put your mind to it,

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:31.137
you can achieve greatness,
it feels good to me.

00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:32.104
 That was pretty awesome.

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:34.640
 It almost killed Gio.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.210
 To see what lethal
damage your blade can do,

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.912
I will deliver killing
blows of slashes and thrusts

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:41.480
on this ballistics dummy.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.483
The sword breaker comes from
the style called main-gauche,

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:46.385
which means "left hand."

00:04:46.485 --> 00:04:48.988
Therefore, I will see the
offensive capabilities of

00:04:49.054 --> 00:04:51.824
your knife with my left hand.

00:04:51.891 --> 00:04:52.725
James, you're up first.

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:53.225
You ready?

00:04:53.326 --> 00:04:54.594
 Let's do it.

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:57.964
[music playing]

00:05:06.505 --> 00:05:07.974
Well, James, as an
offensive weapon,

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.844
it slashes nicely and punctures
easily for a deep cut.

00:05:11.911 --> 00:05:14.781
But at the same time, I
know that sword breakers

00:05:14.881 --> 00:05:16.148
have to be held this way.

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:18.417
It wants to go this way.

00:05:18.517 --> 00:05:23.222
Nonetheless, it cuts, it
stabs, and it will kill.

00:05:23.289 --> 00:05:23.989
Good job.

00:05:24.056 --> 00:05:24.957
 Thank you.

00:05:25.023 --> 00:05:25.824
 Erich, your turn.

00:05:25.891 --> 00:05:26.759
You ready?

00:05:26.859 --> 00:05:28.994
 Yes, sir.

00:05:29.095 --> 00:05:32.465
[music playing]

00:05:38.537 --> 00:05:40.840
 Well, Erich, if I need
to use it offensively,

00:05:40.906 --> 00:05:43.109
it's easy to index and
switch to that edge.

00:05:43.175 --> 00:05:47.012
When I did thrust and slash
out, we got to test the edge,

00:05:47.079 --> 00:05:48.414
and it lacerations.

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:50.849
Your blade will kill.

00:05:50.916 --> 00:05:52.318
 Thank you.

00:05:52.418 --> 00:05:54.686
 Now to test the sharpness
of the edge of your blade,

00:05:54.786 --> 00:05:57.290
I'm going to cut the
rope first with the fish

00:05:57.357 --> 00:06:00.059
and then the meat, our
"Forged in Fire" trifecta.

00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:01.326
[laughter]

00:06:01.393 --> 00:06:02.194
James, you're up first.

00:06:02.294 --> 00:06:02.929
You ready?

00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:05.163
 Yes, sir.

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:08.768
[music playing]

00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:16.175
 Hell yeah.

00:06:16.242 --> 00:06:18.311
 All right, James,
wicked sharp.

00:06:18.411 --> 00:06:21.247
Going from there, the sword
breaker in particular,

00:06:21.347 --> 00:06:22.882
it's a left-handed weapon.

00:06:22.948 --> 00:06:25.451
Now, that ring is to
protect your knuckles.

00:06:28.754 --> 00:06:29.288
Oops.

00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:30.556
 Yes, sir.

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:33.025
 Other than that,
this thing is a beast.

00:06:33.092 --> 00:06:33.992
 Thank you.

00:06:34.092 --> 00:06:35.294
 All right, Erich, let's do it.

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:37.929
OK.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:42.502
[music playing]

00:06:48.908 --> 00:06:51.176
[laughter]

00:06:51.277 --> 00:06:53.646
All right, first
off, that's sharp.

00:06:53.713 --> 00:06:57.382
It just went right through the
rope, right through the fish.

00:06:57.449 --> 00:06:58.450
Didn't cut all the way.

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:00.185
That was me, not the knife.

00:07:00.286 --> 00:07:01.454
You can see the
cuts on that beef.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:02.521
JAMES: Yeah, sure.

00:07:02.587 --> 00:07:05.224
 Just clean, straight through.

00:07:05.324 --> 00:07:07.493
Knuckle guard
above the knuckles.

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:08.727
Crenulations forward.

00:07:08.827 --> 00:07:10.962
It can catch with
that, stop things.

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:12.298
That's great.

00:07:12.365 --> 00:07:14.232
All in all, I think it's
a great-looking piece,

00:07:14.299 --> 00:07:15.934
and it is definitely sharp.

00:07:16.034 --> 00:07:17.336
 Thank you.

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:18.637
 All right, gentlemen,
this is the strength test.

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:20.005
The sword breaker's
teeth were designed

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:23.041
to grab onto an opponent's
weapon and hold on fast.

00:07:23.108 --> 00:07:25.478
Now, to test the strength and
durability of your blades,

00:07:25.545 --> 00:07:27.646
we've hooked them into our
mechanical contraption here.

00:07:27.746 --> 00:07:29.248
And what that piston
is going to do,

00:07:29.315 --> 00:07:31.717
it's going to pull your blade
that way, bending that rapier,

00:07:31.783 --> 00:07:34.520
putting a lot of
torque on your blade.

00:07:34.587 --> 00:07:36.088
Let's see if the
teeth of your weapon

00:07:36.188 --> 00:07:37.423
will hold on to that rapier.

00:07:37.489 --> 00:07:38.691
James, you're up first.
You ready for this?

00:07:38.791 --> 00:07:39.291
 I'm ready.

00:07:39.358 --> 00:07:40.258
Let's do this.

00:07:40.359 --> 00:07:42.862
 3, 2, 1, pull.

00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:50.770
 [bleep] Damn it, man.

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:52.238
[laughter]

00:07:52.338 --> 00:07:53.005
Oh.

00:07:56.809 --> 00:07:58.310
 OK.

00:07:58.377 --> 00:08:02.948
You picked up an
ever-so-slight bend.

00:08:03.048 --> 00:08:04.583
I mean, there was a lot
of pressure on that,

00:08:04.683 --> 00:08:06.818
and you could actually
see that weapon bending.

00:08:06.918 --> 00:08:10.188
You can see what it
did this rapier blade.

00:08:10.255 --> 00:08:11.389
All in all, well done.

00:08:11.456 --> 00:08:12.225
 Thank you.

00:08:12.291 --> 00:08:14.393
 Erich, your turn.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:15.528
 Let's put it to the test.

00:08:15.594 --> 00:08:19.431
 In 3, 2, 1, pull.

00:08:19.498 --> 00:08:22.902
[music playing]

00:08:30.809 --> 00:08:32.878
All right, Erich, what
happened in our test

00:08:32.944 --> 00:08:36.281
here is the shape of
your teeth, the way they

00:08:36.381 --> 00:08:37.783
widen towards the outside--

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:38.984
ERICH: Yep.

00:08:39.051 --> 00:08:41.621
 --it allowed that rapier
to twist and pop out,

00:08:41.687 --> 00:08:45.224
as opposed to when you see these
historically that they have

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:46.926
sort of a head on these teeth.

00:08:47.026 --> 00:08:50.162
So once they go into that
section, it's harder for them

00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:51.430
to escape.

00:08:51.497 --> 00:08:54.100
The fact that this
opening here is so wide,

00:08:54.166 --> 00:08:57.936
it just allows that to happen.

00:08:58.036 --> 00:08:58.971
 Sure.

00:08:59.038 --> 00:09:00.506
 Other than that,
I mean, it held up.

00:09:00.572 --> 00:09:03.175
You bent the rapier
right until it popped.

00:09:03.242 --> 00:09:06.879
Your blade stayed true,
even after all that flexing,

00:09:06.946 --> 00:09:10.016
but those wide teeth seem
to be a bit of an issue.

00:09:10.082 --> 00:09:13.419
 My blade grabbed the rapier
and torqued it better than his,

00:09:13.519 --> 00:09:15.554
but his sword didn't warp.

00:09:15.654 --> 00:09:17.123
Mine did slightly.

00:09:17.189 --> 00:09:19.691
So we're still neck and neck.

00:09:19.758 --> 00:09:23.362
 James, Erich, this was
your shot at redemption.

00:09:23.462 --> 00:09:24.863
However, in this
arena of competition,

00:09:24.963 --> 00:09:26.531
there can only be one champion.

00:09:26.598 --> 00:09:28.834
The "Forged in
Fire" champion is--

00:09:33.071 --> 00:09:34.373
James.

00:09:34.473 --> 00:09:35.974
 [bleep].

00:09:36.074 --> 00:09:37.276
Damn it.

00:09:37.343 --> 00:09:38.243
Congratulations.

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:39.478
 Well, thank you very much.

00:09:39.578 --> 00:09:42.448
 Erich, please
surrender your blade.

00:09:42.514 --> 00:09:44.049
 I agree with the
judge's decision.

00:09:44.149 --> 00:09:46.652
I had some aggressive
choosing in the club pattern

00:09:46.719 --> 00:09:47.953
for the blade.

00:09:48.053 --> 00:09:49.822
But last episode I was
on, I definitely did not

00:09:49.888 --> 00:09:51.823
show what I'm capable of doing.

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:54.526
This time, I was
able to put forward

00:09:54.593 --> 00:09:56.295
a competition-worthy blade.

00:09:56.361 --> 00:09:57.964
So that's redemption in itself.

00:10:00.466 --> 00:10:02.068
 James, congratulations.

00:10:02.134 --> 00:10:04.637
You've redeemed yourself, and
you're the "Forged in Fire"

00:10:04.704 --> 00:10:08.007
champion who also receives
that check for $10,000.

00:10:08.074 --> 00:10:08.941
How do you feel?

00:10:09.008 --> 00:10:10.876
 Oh, man, it's
been a long road.

00:10:10.943 --> 00:10:12.811
I'll tell you what, I
have redeemed myself.

00:10:12.878 --> 00:10:14.747
 All right, James,
please present

00:10:14.847 --> 00:10:17.383
your weapon to the judges.

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:18.884
 It's much more fun
to get to present

00:10:18.951 --> 00:10:20.986
your weapon to the judges
than having to surrender it.

00:10:21.052 --> 00:10:22.654
Two years ago, being
out in the first round,

00:10:22.754 --> 00:10:24.189
then making it to the
championship round

00:10:24.256 --> 00:10:27.360
and then now even
winning, and it's-- this

00:10:27.460 --> 00:10:28.793
is truly redemption.

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:30.463
My daughters will be proud.

00:10:30.529 --> 00:10:32.164
They can tell their
friends, hey, my dad's

00:10:32.231 --> 00:10:33.431
a "Forged in Fire" champion.

00:10:33.532 --> 00:10:37.637
[music playing]

